  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "2bc29c96",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Observations\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Training accuracy climbed steadily from ~59% to ~98% over 10 epochs, while validation accuracy plateaued much earlier, around 70–77%. This gap is a clear sign of **overfitting** — the model started memorizing training images rather than learning generalizable features, especially visible from epoch 6 onward where training loss kept dropping but validation loss started rising.\n",
    "2. Final test accuracy was **77.1%**, with precision (0.79) slightly higher than recall (0.74), meaning the model is a bit more conservative about labelling something a dog — it misses a few more true dogs (131 false negatives) than it wrongly flags cats as dogs (98 false positives).\n",
    "3. The confusion matrix shows both classes are reasonably balanced in errors, so the model isn't biased heavily toward one class; it's a fairly even mix of cat-as-dog and dog-as-cat mistakes.\n",
    "4. Given only a plain CNN (no data augmentation, no dropout, no transfer learning) trained on a modest subset of images, this performance is reasonable but has clear room for improvement — techniques like data augmentation, dropout layers, or transfer learning (e.g. MobileNet/VGG16) would likely close the train/validation gap and push accuracy higher.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "269aae0d",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Task 5: Conclusion\n",
    "\n",
    "This project built a CNN to classify cat and dog images, reaching 77% test accuracy after 10 epochs, though training accuracy (98%) outpaced validation accuracy, showing overfitting on the training set used. Convolutional layers are essential because they automatically learn spatial features, such as edges, textures and shapes, directly from raw pixels, removing the need for manual feature engineering. Pooling layers then downsample these feature maps, reducing the parameter count and making the model more tolerant of small shifts or distortions. Compared to a plain ANN, a CNN's key advantage is that it preserves an image's 2D spatial structure through shared convolutional filters, rather than flattening pixels into one long vector and losing that structure. A limitation of CNNs is that they need large amounts of labeled data to generalize well; with limited data, as seen here, they can overfit quickly and lose accuracy on unseen images."
   ]
  }
